{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the lost-abort repair at head 09c7ac57015f903f238d1a0eaf595d9422da7660, tree f4873387, for request 2fd85d18 and review request 89126c91. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds five paths to main b1069bf1. `#stream` now takes the `Request` and reads `request.signal.aborted` at the existing check. Nothing else in the path changes.\n\n## Your four points\n\n1. The trace is enough to falsify the test-race hypothesis. The server's controller aborted 1 ms after the client, and the check read `false` 108 ms later, so the 100 ms sleep wasn't too short. Collection on the failing server path stays inferred, as the note says. The plain Node result (0 of 20 and 20 of 20), the witness and the whole-owner counts support that inference. The counts of 5 of 24 and 0 of 24 are not strong on their own, and I read them only together with the other results.\n\n2. The witness fails for the stated reason. I tested this in a scratch clone:\n   - With the fix reverted, the case failed 2 of 2 runs with `expected 200 to be 204`.\n   - With the fix reverted and the forced collection removed, it passed 3 of 3 runs. So on the old code the failure needs the collection, and the abort is what gets lost.\n   - As landed, the whole owner passed 10 of 10 twice.\n   The case doesn't run through `node.ts`, because it drops the caller's reference directly. That's a fair way to make collection certain, and it doesn't reproduce the server's own optimized frame. The new case can't fail on correct code through collection timing, because the fixed code holds the `Request` until the check.\n\n3. The forced collection is acceptable in this file. `setFlagsFromString(\"--expose-gc\")` after start-up is the case Node warns about in general, but this flag only adds `gc` to newly created contexts. It stays set for the rest of that test process, and nothing in this file depends on it being absent. A later cleanup could move it to the project's `execArgv`. I don't ask for that here.\n\n4. The seal adoption is sound. The source diff is one call, one parameter, one check and a comment. It adds no route or method and changes no gate class. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 of 10. Gate 1 at a8034631 stays failed, in `gate-1-failed`.\n\n## What I also checked\n\n- Line 387 is the only reader of a request's signal in `http.ts`, and no await sits between it and the install. Abort after install reaches the stream through its response cancel, as before.\n- The other production holder of `request.signal` is `packages/checkers/src/container-origin.ts:35`, which you already asked about in fb3f71f5. This head doesn't cover it, and it doesn't need to.\n- Gate 2 at 731bbfd8, tree c9e1acb0, exits 0 with all stages passing. Its `test.log` shows Node 95, Counting 56, Plain 3,203 with one skip and Native 889 with two skips. Its form is a plain `npm run gate` log, not a process receipt, and that is enough for a head of this size.\n- From 731bbfd8 to this head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- The evidence verifies. All 16 manifest rows match by size and digest, the directory has 57 files, its `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 8abafe00, and no file is writable. The manifest has digest e14a2683. The diagnosis unit has 10 files and reruns clean with digest 67cfbd79.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main b1069bf1 gives tree f4873387. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `check-commit-messages` checks 3 with 0 flagged, `check-doc-links` on an export finds 775 links with 0 broken, and `git diff --check` is clean. The worktree stayed clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. The workerd exposure is a single probe of a constructed `Request`, with no forced collection and no test of an incoming request's signal. The note says so. The fix reads the same signal there, so it can't make that path worse.\n2. Whether `enable_request_signal` is needed for a hosted abort at all stays unestablished. It deserves its own look if hosted legacy streams matter.\n3. The reproducing command's descriptor limit and reporter were not separated. The note says so.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe fix keeps the object that owns the link, and it adds no state. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nThe defect could end a live stream for a client that had gone. It touches no authority and no stored data. The fix doesn't widen anything. Approved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe source change is three lines, and the note explains why the fixture-only route was dropped. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: the repair, the witness, the gate and the evidence hold. No deployment follows, and Workers stay on 112eff12 until J4 closes.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1a4ef5be7958efe39ed289edf922baad02939d5b","binding":"assigned","head":"09c7ac57015f903f238d1a0eaf595d9422da7660","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5e88345c1c9c07b21355bfe8d9a9f24d34bd5507\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a001b0fe0acbc9f8a96eeeb4563e05b427a36710\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5e88345c1c9c07b21355bfe8d9a9f24d34bd5507","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0ce79ab8af87a0c6455e2a648e4acd5fbfec26b5, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:13cff675f846478a321b80253b66da87d307c52a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1a4ef5be7958efe39ed289edf922baad02939d5b, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5e88345c1c9c07b21355bfe8d9a9f24d34bd5507, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:89126c91790bdfa474dc90b82835a82a366995fb, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c5680e8b5cd1745b57882b96ea5d819d5dbdba1f, git:sha1:589bc